{
  "difficulty": "easy",
  "score": 3,
  "estimated_minutes": 15,
  "factors": {
    "transaction_background": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "The reviewer needs only the distinction between the Griffin insurance claim, Rosen’s later bad-faith action, and the $350,000 settlement whose confidentiality restrictions Rosen seeks to enforce."
    },
    "domain_knowledge": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "The merger-clause and confidentiality disputes turn on ordinary contract interpretation, not specialised knowledge of E&O insurance or Georgia RICO law."
    },
    "defect_spread": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "Understanding requires reading the confidentiality provision alongside paragraph 4’s entire-agreement language and the settlement’s exchange of payment and releases, all expressly discussed together by the court."
    },
    "court_reasoning": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "Lines 60–65 expressly identify both parties’ readings and hold that the writing is integrated and protects contractual terms rather than the underlying lawsuits’ existence or facts."
    },
    "issue_distinctness": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "Although d01 and d02 share reasoning, the existence and effect of the merger clause are readily distinguished from the scope of 'terms of this Release and the terms of the settlement of this claim.'"
    }
  },
  "hardest_defect": "d02 — The reviewer must distinguish the written settlement’s contractual terms from the underlying litigation facts and alleged oral conditions, while understanding how the merger clause supports that distinction. The court explains that boundary directly and illustrates it through the payment and releases.",
  "reason": "This case is suitable for a generalist reviewer after a short contextual read. The central dispute is whether Zurich promised to protect only the settlement terms or also the existence and facts of the earlier lawsuits. The reviewer should focus on the settlement provisions and lines 60–65, using the later breach analysis to confirm why the settlement amount differs from ordinary claims history. The effective-date dispute, insurance-enrollment allegations, fraud claims, and RICO discussion need not be mastered to recognise these court defects."
}
